## a b s t r a c t
Recently, the developments of MOFs and their applications on heavy metal removal have attracted much attention. In this paper, we focused on the influence of co-existing ions on the adsorbability of epigallocatechin gallate (EGCG) modified ZIF-8 in a multi-metal system (Cd(II), $\mathsf{C u}(\mathrm{II})$ , Ni(II), and $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ ). It was found that the adsorption of Cd(II), $\mathsf{C u}(\mathrm{II})$ , Ni(II), and $\mathsf{C r}(\mathsf{V I})$ on the ZIF-8-EGCG fitted well with the pseudo second-order kinetic model and Langmuir isotherm. The adsorption selectivity of ZIF-8-EGCG in the mixed metal solution followed the order of $\mathrm{Cu(II)}\gg\mathrm{Cr(VI)}>\mathrm{Cd(II)}>\mathrm{Ni(II)}.$ Besides, $\mathsf{C u}(\mathrm{II})$ was the most attractive substance with a maximum capacity of $232.97\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . Copper also disturbed the adsorption of $\operatorname{Cd}(\operatorname{II})$ and $\mathsf{N i}(\boldsymbol{\mathrm{II}})$ and contributed the most to the $\mathsf{C r}(\mathsf{V I})$ removal. The natural anions (e.g., $\mathsf{C l}^{-}$ , $\mathsf{N O}_{3}^{-}$ , $\mathsf{A s O}_{2}^{-}$ , $S0_{4}^{2-\cdot}$ significantly impeded $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ adsorption, while low concentrations of natural cations (e.g., $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ , $\mathsf{N a}^{+}$ , ${\mathsf{C a}}^{2+}$ , and $\mathsf{M g}^{2+}$ ) facilitated the adsorption of Cd(II), $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ and $\mathsf{N i}(\mathrm{II})$ .
