## 1. Introduction
Water pollution is recognized as one of the most prevalent environmental problems globally [1–3], and heavy metals are one of the most active and diverse pollutants in the water system [3,4]. It is well known that many heavy metals (e.g., cadmium (Cd), copper (Cu), nickel (Ni), and chromium (Cr)), which may exist in various forms, have extensive industrial sources and various biological toxicity [2,3,5,6]. At present, adsorption is considered the most feasible method because of its low investment cost, eco-friendliness, and simple operation [3,7].
Nowadays, metal-organic frame materials (MOFs) have attracted much attention because of their advantages derived from both organic and inorganic components [2,8,9]. Related MOF research has extended in various fields such as biomedicine, chemical materials, energy storage, gas separation, and pollution control [2,9,10]. Abdollahi et al. [11] found that the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}@\mathrm{TMU}-32$ had high capacities for ${\mathrm{Hg}}({\mathrm{II}})$ $\cdot905\ \mathrm{mg}.\mathbf{g}^{-1};$ and $\mathsf{P b}(\mathrm{II})$ $(1600~\mathrm{mgcdotg^{-1}}).$ removal in aqueous solution. Lv and co-workers [12] presented that a MOF $\mathrm{'MIL}{-}101{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2})$ sensing platform had an efficient removal of $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ , ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ , and $\mathsf{P b}^{2+}$ from water. Elaiwi et al. [13] successfully used an amine-functionalized MIL-101(Cr) to remove Cu (II) $(69.9\mathrm{\mgcdotg^{-1}};$ ) and $\operatorname{Cd}(\operatorname{II})$ $\left(63.15\mathrm{\mg}.\mathbf{g}^{-1}\right).$ ) from an aqueous environment. In our previous work [14], we successfully synthesized an epigallocatechin gallate (EGCG) modified MOF material called ZIF-8-EGCG. The as-formed ZIF-8-EGCG had superior physicochemical stability and the ability to remove and reduce $\mathbf{\mathop{Cr}}(\mathsf{V I})$ . Nevertheless, the metal competition process (or metal selectivity) in the mixed heavy metal solution has rarely been studied in the MOF adsorption studies.
In natural contaminated water, there are plentiful geochemical anions (e.g., chlorine $(\mathsf{C l}^{-})$ , nitrate $(\mathsf{N O}_{3}^{-})$ , arsenite $(\mathsf{A s O}_{2}^{-})$ ), and sulfate $(\mathsf{S O}_{4}^{2-})_{.}^{.}$ ) and cations (e.g., potassium $(\boldsymbol{\mathrm{K}}^{+})$ , sodium $\left(\mathsf{N a}^{+}\right)$ , calcium $(\mathsf{C a}^{2+})$ , and magnesium $(\mathrm{Mg}^{2+})_{\perp}^{.}$ ) [15]. They may exert various effects (improve or inhibit) on the adsorption of the target heavy metal ions. For example, Yang et al. [16] concluded that the co-existing ions (i.e., $\mathsf{C l}^{-}$ , $S0_{4}^{2-}$ , and $\mathsf{P O}_{4}^{3-}$ ) could compete with Cr (VI) for the active sites on MnUiO-66, while Li and co-workers [17] discovered that a silver-triazole MOF-1- ${\bf\cdot N O}_{3}$ had a preference for $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}0_{7}^{2-}$ over $S0_{4}^{2-}$ , $\mathsf{N O}_{3}^{-}$ , ${\mathsf{C l O}}_{4}^{-}$ and $\mathsf{C l^{-}}$ . Although the application of MOFs in the water treatment has been very popular, current research is confined in a routine study of a single factor on adsorption capacity, i.e., reusability, metal concentration, temperature, $\mathsf{p H}$ and material dosage [10,18]. Therefore, the influence of coexisting natural ions on the adsorption of heavy metals is the key to testing whether the material can be practically used in the water treatment.
Hence, in this study, we intended to investigate 1) the adsorption behavior of Cd(II), Cu(II), Ni(II), or $\mathsf{C r}(\mathsf{V I})$ on the ZIF-8-EGCG material; 2) the adsorption characteristics when multiple heavy metals co-exist; and 3) the effects of inorganic cations/anions on the metal removal process.
