In the batch studies, the selectivity of ZIF-8-EGCG towards heavy metals (i.e., Cd(II), Cu(II), Ni(II) and $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ ) and the influence of divalent heavy metal cations (i.e., Cd(II), $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ , and Ni(II)) on Cr (VI) removal were investigated. The simulated polluted solutions consisted of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ and one of the divalent metal ions (marked as $\mathrm{Cr}+\mathrm{Cd}/\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{Ni})$ . The multi-metal solution of more than two heavy metals was split into $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ -containing mixture (i.e., ${\mathrm{Cr}}+{\mathrm{Cd}}+{\mathrm{C}}$ ${\bf u}+{\bf N i}_{.}^{\cdot}$ ) and $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ -free mixture (i.e., $\mathsf{C d}+\mathsf{C u}+\mathsf{N i}_{,}$ ). The adsorption condition was equal to the above adsorption experiment (material dosage: $0.5\:\mathrm{g}.\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ , $\mathsf{p H}=4$ , shaking time: $24\mathrm{h}$ ). The initial concentration of each heavy metal in the single metal solution and the mixed metal solution was $100\ \mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ .
## 2.4. Effect of co-existing ions on heavy metal adsorption
The ZIF-8-EGCG $_{(0.01\ \mathrm{g})}$ was mixed with $20~{\mathrm{mL}}~25~{\mathrm{mg}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{L}}^{-1}~{\mathrm{Cr}}$ (VI) solution in $50~\mathrm{mL}$ conical flasks, accompanied by one natural common anion such as $\subset{}^{-}$ , $\mathsf{N O}_{3}^{-}$ , $\mathsf{A s O}_{2}^{-}$ , and $S0_{4}^{2-}$ (concentration of 1, 10, and $25\ \mathrm{mg}.\mathrm{L}^{-1}.$ ); or the ZIF-8-EGCG $_{(0.01\ \mathrm{g})}$ was mixed with $20~\mathrm{mL}$ $\_{100}\mathrm{\mg.L^{-1}C d(I I)/C u(I I)/N i(I I)}$ solution, accompanied by one common cation such as $\operatorname{K}^{+}$ , ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ , ${\mathsf{C a}}^{2+}$ , and $\mathsf{M g}^{2+}$ (concentration of 1, 10, and $25~\mathrm{mg}.\mathrm{L}^{-1}.$ ). The $\mathsf{p H}$ was fixed at 4, and the batch adsorption followed the same procedure as mentioned above.
## 2.5. Characterization and data analysis
The concentration of ${\mathsf{C d}}({\mathsf{I I}}),{\mathsf{C u}}({\mathsf{I I}}),$ and Ni(II) was measured on an atomic absorption spectrometer (AAS, PinAAcle 900F, PerkinElmer). The $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ concentration was measured by UV–vis spectrophotometer (UV-2550, Shimadzu) through 1,5-diphenyl carbazide method at $540\mathrm{nm}$ . In each of the above adsorption studies, the removal capacity of ZIF-8-EGCG was calculated as:
$$
\mathrm{removal~capacity}(\mathrm{mg}\cdot\mathbf{g}^{-1})={\frac{(C_{o}-C_{e})\times V}{m}}
$$
where $C_{\mathrm{o}}$ and $C_{\mathrm{e}}(\mathrm{mg.L}^{-1})$ is the initial concentration and the equilibrium concentration of the simulated solution, respectively; $V$ $\mathrm{(mL)}$ is the initial volume brought into the conical flask; $m$ (g) is the dosage of ZIF-8-EGCG.
The XRD spectrum was analyzed by Jade 6 (Java Agent Development Framework 6, TILAB). Office 2016 (Microsoft Corporation) was used to integrate data. Origin 2019b 64Bit (OriginLab) was used to perform data conversion and chart analysis. Figure modification and integration were done by Photoshop (Adobe Systems Software, Ireland Ltd).
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Material characterization
The synthesized ZIF-8-EGCG was characterized by SEM and XRD. It was clear that the morphology of the material was spherical with particle diameter ${\sim}31~\mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 2a). The crystallinity of the material maintained the structure of ZIF-8 with signature peaks at (10 0), (110), (111), and (200) (Fig. 2b) [14,19]. The BET surface area and average pore size was $113.02\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ and $7.12\ \mathrm{nm}$ , respectively. As shown in Fig. 2c, the FT-IR absorption peaks facilitated the identification of functional groups on ZIF-8-EGCG (i.e., imidazole ring stretching (NAH): $600{-}1500\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , phenolic hydroxyl group (– OH): $3381.56~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ $:1632.13~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , and CAH: 3135.77 and $2929.00~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ) [14,20,21]. The zeta potential of ZIF-8-EGCG indicated the negatively changed surface (Fig. 2d). According to our previous work [14], it has been proven that the EGCG was first adsorbed to the surface of ZIF-8 and then gradually replaced the original imidazole ligand from the outside to the inside of ZIF-8.
## 3.2. Kinetics adsorption analysis
Preliminary adsorption experiment showed that the removal capacity of ZIF-8 towards Cd(II), $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ , Ni(II), and $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ was inferior to that of ZIF-8-EGCG (Table S1), indicating that the EGCG modification significantly improved the overall adsorption capacity of the original ZIF-8, irrespective of the metal that was studied. The difference was remarkable in the adsorption of Ni(II) and Cr (VI).
The adsorption behavior of $\mathsf{C d}(\mathrm{II}),\mathsf{C u}(\mathrm{II}),\mathsf{N i}(\mathrm{II}),$ and $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ on the ZIF-8-EGCG was also studied in the kinetics adsorption experiments (Fig. 3). Pseudo first-order and pseudo second-order models are related to adsorption controlling steps regulated by physisorption and chemisorption, respectively [22]. Obviously, the pseudo second-order kinetic model $R^{2}$ ranging from 0.9039 to 0.9308, Table 1) fit the metal adsorption on ZIF-8-EGCG better than the pseudo first-order kinetic model ( $R^{2}$ ranging from 0.8330 to 0.8497, Table 1). It indicated that chemisorption (including ion exchange and chemical bond reactions) had a greater contribution than physisorption (mainly electrostatic attraction) to the adsorption at the solid-liquid interface [23]. In the pseudo second-order model, the adsorption capacity for the metals at equilibrium $(Q_{e})$ followed the order of $\mathrm{Cu}\left(221.75~\mathrm{mg}.\mathbf{g}^{-1}\right)>\mathrm{Cd}$ $(99.55\ \mathrm{mg}.\mathbf{g}^{-1})>\mathrm{Ni}$ $(72.67\ \mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1})>\mathrm{Cr}(12.28\ \mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1})$ . The adsorption rate was fast in the first $25~\mathrm{{min}}$ , and then gradually slowed down until it reached equilibrium (i.e., within $100~\mathrm{{min}}$ for Ni(II) and $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ ; within 300 min for Cd(II) and $\mathsf{C u}(\mathrm{II})\mathrm{.}$ . The initial rapid adsorption rate was attributed to the numerous accessible active adsorption sites on the surface of ZIF-8-EGCG [24]. In particular, $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ had the highest $k_{2}$ value (0.02313), followed by Ni(II) $(4.939\times10^{-3})$ , $\mathsf{C u}(\mathrm{II})$ $(3.254\times10^{-4})$ , and Cd(II) $(5.453\times10^{-4})$ . The highest $k_{2}$ indicated the fastest adsorption of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ onto the ZIF-8-EGCG, which is owing to the quick chelation and reduction between $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ and phenolic hydroxyl groups (–OH) on the ZIF-8-EGCG [14]. Moreover, more protonated N produced on the ZIF-8-EGCG could also contribute to the fast adsorption of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ [14]. In contrast, the electrostatic attraction between the cationic metals and ZIF-8-EGCG, and the ion exchange between the cationic metal (i.e., Cu, Cd, and Ni) and Zn (metal nodes of ZIF-8-EGCG) may contribute to the slower adsorption process than $\mathsf{C r}(\mathsf{V I})$ [25].
## 3.3. Isothermal adsorption analysis
Results of ZIF-8-EGCG adsorption capacity demonstrated that the adsorption capacity increased with increasing concentration of the metal ion before reaching the equilibrium (Fig. 4). The maximum adsorption capacity $(Q_{\mathrm{m}})$ followed the order of $\mathsf{C u}(\mathrm{II})\gg\mathsf{C d}$ $(\mathrm{II})>\mathsf{N i}(\mathrm{II})>\mathsf{C r}(\mathsf{V I})$ (Table 2). ZIF-8-EGCG had a remarkable adsorption capacity for Cu $(232.97~\mathrm{mgcdotg^{-1}}^{\cdot}$ . It can be attributed to the high electronegativity of Cu (2.0) and the similar atomic radius between $Z\mathrm{n}$ and $\mathtt{C u}$ ; hence the Zn in ZIF-8-EGCG can be easily replaced by $\mathtt{C u}$ through ion exchange [25,26]. According to the value of $R^{2}$ (Table 2), the Langmuir isotherm was better in describing the adsorption procedure, indicating a uniform attachment of metal ions onto the spherical ZIF-8-EGCG surface (i.e., monolayer adsorption) [22,27]. Simultaneously, the value of $R_{L}$ was much less than 1, indicating that ZIF-8-EGCG had favorable adsorption and abundant active sites [24,28]. The values of $1/n$ ranging from 0.2644 to 0.6144 proposed that the process was dominated by chemical reaction (Table 2) [27,29].
## 3.4. Adsorption behavior in multi-metal mixture
To find out the competitive behavior of cationic metal ions and the influence of metal cations on $\mathsf{C r}(\mathsf{V I})$ removal process, $100\ \mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ mixed metal solution containing different combinations of $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ , Cd(II), Ni(II) and $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ was loaded into a $50~\mathrm{mL}$ conical flask with $_{0.01\mathrm{~g~}}$ ZIF-8-EGCG. Interestingly, the morphology of ZIF-8-EGCG in the mixed metal solution changed from a small spherical shape to a withered leaf after adsorption (Fig. 5a and b), indicating that the external environment had caused the morphological change of ZIF-8-EGCG. Zhang et al. [25] reported that the ion exchange between ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ and $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ caused the structural transformation of ZIF-8 after ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ adsorption. Meantime, Zhou et al. [30] found that the ZIF-8 surface became rounder and smoother after exposure to the contaminant mixture (i.e., Cu and norfloxacin), possibly due to ion exchange, $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking, and electrostatic interactions. Therefore, the change in the morphology of ZIF-8-EGCG could be attributed to the ion-exchange process between Zn and the studied metals, and the complexation reaction between the metal ions and the active sites on ZIF-8-EGCG (i.e., – NH, and –OH) [25,30–32].
The selectivity and competitiveness between the metal cations were given in Figs. 5 and 6a. Copper was the most attractive metal whether it was in a single or mixed solution, with the removal capacity ranging from $154.60~\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ to $190.30~\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , followed by Cd(II) (from $16.20~\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ to $97.80~\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ ) and $\mathsf{N i}(\boldsymbol{\mathrm{II}})$ (from $6.90~\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ to $63.30~\mathrm{mg\cdotg^{-1}}.$ (Fig. 6a), which matched with the order of adsorption isotherm. In the mixed metal solution, cocompetition could occur if multiple cations are bound by limited surface active sites [8]. Interestingly, the adsorption of $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ only dropped by $15\%$ in the presence of $\operatorname{Cd}(\operatorname{II})$ and $\mathsf{N i}(\mathrm{II})$ , while that of $\operatorname{Cd}(\operatorname{II})$ and Ni(II) dropped significantly to $78\%$ and $72\%$ when $\mathtt{C u}$ (II) was added, respectively. It could be clearly seen from the SEM images (Fig. 5) that only $Z\mathrm{n}$ (used to construct ZIF-8-EGCG) existed in the ZIF-8-EGCG before the adsorption reaction. After adsorption, the distribution density of Cu was the largest on the material, while that of Zn was significantly weakened due to ion-exchange [30,33].
The impact of $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ , Cd(II), and $\mathsf{N i}(\boldsymbol{\mathrm{II}})$ on the removal of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ by the ZIF-8-EGCG was presented in Fig. 6b. Interestingly, the removal capacity of $\mathsf{C r}(\mathsf{V I})$ $(22.46~\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1})$ increased after $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ $(46.40~\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}.$ ), Cd(II) $(27.40~\mathrm{mg}\cdot\mathbf{g}^{-1},$ ), Ni(II) $(26.93\ \mathrm{mg}.\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ ), and the above three metals $(47.87\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ ) was/were added to the solution. Again, $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ contributed the most to the removal of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ . It is assumed that the adsorbed metal cations could provide a contact bridge between the negatively charged ZIF-8-EGCG (Fig. 1d) and Cr (VI), hence facilitating the removal of $\mathrm{cr}$ anions [15]. It is admirable for practical application that the $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ removal was remarkably increased in the co-contaminated wastewater containing cations such as $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ , Cd(II), and Ni(II).
Furthermore, the adsorption selectivity $(\alpha_{B}^{A})$ [32] was used to evaluate the affinity of the heavy metals on the ZIF-8-EGCG, which is expressed by the following equation:
$$
\alpha_{B}^{A}=\frac{(100-R_{A}\times100)}{(100-R_{B}\times100)}
$$
where $R_{A}$ and $R_{B}$ is the removal rate of ion A and ion B, respectively. In our work, $\mathbf{\mathop{Cr}}(\mathsf{V I})$ was selected as ion A to study the interference of other heavy metal ions (ion B) on the selectivity of ZIF-8-EGCG. The $\alpha_{B}^{A}$ of ZIF-8-EGCG for $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ in the single solution (only $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}.$ and binary mixture $(\mathrm{Cr+Cd/Cu/Ni})$ were listed as the ideal and mixture selectivity, respectively (Table 3). The influence of $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ on the adsorption of $\mathsf{C r}(\mathsf{V I})$ by ZIF-8-EGCG was far more significant than that of $\operatorname{Cd}(\operatorname{II})$ and $\mathsf{N i}(\mathrm{II})$ , suggesting the specific affinity of $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ on the ZIF-8-EGCG. Furthermore, it also implied that the adsorption preference of cationic heavy metals by ZIF-8-EGCG following the order of $\mathrm{Cu(II)}\gg\mathrm{Cd(II)}>\mathrm{Ni(II)},$ , which corresponded to the aforementioned isothermal and kinetics results.
## 3.5. The impact of $p H$ on $C r(W)$ removal
The solution pH had a significant effect on the adsorption of Cr (VI) by the ZIF-8-EGCG (Fig. 6c) [14,37]. Not surprisingly, the best removal capacity was found at pH 4 $16.65~\mathrm{mg}\cdot\mathbf{g}^{-1}.$ ), followed by 7 $(10.17~\mathrm{mg}.\mathbf{g}^{-1}.$ ) and 9 $(8.90\ \mathrm{mgcdotg^{-1}}$ ), which matched with the zeta potential trend of ZIF-8-EGCG at the three pHs. The surface of ZIF8-EGCG had the least negative charge at $\mathsf{p H}=4$ (Fig. 1d), thereby causing smaller electrical repulsion against $\mathsf{C r}(\mathsf{V I})$ than that at $\mathsf{p H}=7$ or 9 [14].
## 3.6. The impact of natural ions on heavy metals removal
To imitate practical application conditions, different concentrations (1, 10, and $25\ \mathrm{mg}.\mathrm{L}^{-1},$ of $\mathsf{C l}^{-}$ , $\mathsf{N O}_{3}^{-}$ , $A s O_{2}^{-}$ , and $S0_{4}^{2-}$ were applied in a simulated solution which contained $25~\mathrm{mg}.\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ of Cr (VI). Simultaneously, the effect of different concentrations (1, 10, and $25~\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ ) of $\operatorname{K}^{+}$ , ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ , ${\mathsf{C a}}^{2+}$ , and $\mathsf{M g}^{2+}$ in the mixed metal solution (i.e., $100\ \mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ of $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ , Cd(II), and Ni(II)) was investigated.
Within the experimental concentration range, the common cations only had a slight impact on the removal capacity of $\mathtt{C u}$ (II), Cd(II), and Ni(II). It was found that some low concentrations of cations (i.e., K10, Na1, Na10, Ca1, and Ca10 for $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ ; K1, Ca1, Mg1, and $\mathrm{Mg}10$ for Cd(II); K1, K10, Na1, Na10, and Ca1 for Ni(II)) could increase their removal capacity (Fig. 7a–c). Similarly, Zhao et al. [34] reported that the adsorption of $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ on the bentonite-polyacrylamide composites increased with decreasing ionic strength $(\mathsf{N a N O}_{3}$ : from $0.1\mathrm{~M~}$ to 0.001 M). The more easily influenced adsorption at low concentrations may be due to the formation of outer-sphere complexes, which greatly depends on ionic strength [35,36]. Zhao et al. [34] suggested that the charge of the oxygen-containing groups was not compensated by the formation of salt bonds with light metal cations in the diluted solution, which results in the bond formation with the heavy metal cations instead. Besides, the electronegativities of the natural cations are lower than the heavy metal cations (value of electronegativity for K, Na, Ca, Mg, Cu, Cd, and Ni are 0.82, 0.93, 1.00, 1.31, 2.0, 1.7, and 1.8, respectively) [26,37]. Therefore, the heavy metal ions (i.e., Cu, Cd, and Ni) could occupy the active sites more easily than the natural cations (i.e., K, Na, Ca, and Mg). The reduced removal of $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ and Ni(II) was only observed at the highest Ca concentration $(25\mathrm{\mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1}).$ , due to the increased competitiveness and the strong inhibition effects of Ca [38]. It is because the $\mathsf{C a-O H}$ bond is more stable than ${\mathrm{K/Na}}{\mathrm{-OH}}$ bonds, thus leading to a stronger interaction with the active sites [38–42].
Compared to the control group (CK), anions $\mathbf{Cl}^{-}$ , $\mathsf{N O}_{3}^{-}$ , $S0_{4}^{2-}$ and $\mathsf{A s O}_{2}^{-}$ all had a significantly negative effect on the process of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ removal, suggesting their competitiveness. In particular, $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ removal capacity decreased with the increased concentration of $\mathsf{A s O}_{2}^{-}$ $\cdot1{-}10\ \mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1})$ (Fig. 7d). Besides, the ions’ Gibbs energy of hydration, following the order of $\mathrm{Cl^{-}~(-270~{\ k J}{\cdot}m o l^{-1})~<~N O_{3}^{-}}$ $(-300\mathrm{~\boldmath~\kJ}\cdot\mathrm{mol}^{-1})<50_{4}^{2-}$ ( $\begin{array}{r l r}{\mathrm{~:~}1080}&{{}\mathrm{~kJ}{\cdot}\mathsf{m o l}^{-1})}&{<}&{{}\mathrm{Cr}0_{4}^{2-}}\end{array}$ $(-1120\mathrm{kJ}{\cdot}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}).$ , can affect the adsorption preference in the converse order because the lower hydration energy is believed to be more favorable for binding sites [43,44]. Therefore, the as-formed ZIF-8-EGCG material is not suitable for salted metal-contaminated solutions, considering the above results.
## 3.7. Removal mechanisms
The removal mechanisms for cationic heavy metals $(\mathsf{C u}(\mathrm{II})$ Cd (II), and Ni(II)) and anionic $\mathbf{\mathop{Cr}}(\mathsf{V I})$ were different. The superior removal capacity of $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ , Cd(II), and Ni(II) by ZIF-8-EGCG can be attributable to both physical and chemical reactions. The electrostatic attraction between the negatively charged ZIF-8-EGCG and the cations played an important role in the process. Besides, chemical reactions also occurred during the adsorption through ion exchange (cationic heavy metals can replace the $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ node) and coordination (between cations and function groups –NH– and – OH) [25]. Yang et al. [33] suggested that the ion exchange process occurring between the ZIF-8 metal central Zn and adsorbate Cu reveals enormous potential for heavy metal removal in water purification, same as in the study of Zhou et al. [30]. However, we speculate the use of ZIF-based material for cationic metal removal in water purification because the replaced Zn by other cations in the solution (especially Cu) may need further attention in treatment. The removal of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ depended more on the strong chelation with the phenolic hydroxyl group on EGCG. The protonated N produced on the ZIF-8-EGCG could also attract more Cr (VI) for adsorption. Liu et al. [45] pointed out that the EGCG quinone, which has two hydrogen atoms removed from the trihydroxyphenyl B ring of the EGCG molecule, was identified as one of the oxidation products of EGCG during its reaction with $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ . Therefore, the intermediate product quinone and the N from imidazole all contributed to the reduction of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ [14].
## 4. Conclusion
In this study, the adsorbability of ZIF-8-EGCG towards multiheavy metals in aqueous solutions was studied. External natural competing ions were also studied to evaluate the removal capacity of heavy metals by the novel ZIF-8-EGCG nanoparticles in complex conditions. In the metal mixed solution, $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ showed the strongest attraction to ZIF-8-EGCG with the maximum adsorption capacity of $232.97~\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , and the adsorption selectivity of ZIF8-EGCG followed $\mathrm{Cu(II)}\gg\mathrm{Cr(VI)}>\mathrm{Cd(II)}>\mathrm{Ni(II)}.$ . The addition of cations, specifically $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ , could improve the removal of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ (from $22.46~\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ to $46.40~\mathrm{mg\cdotg^{-1}}.$ . However, the leached Zn from the nanomaterial during the ion exchange process may require additional treatment. The studied natural anions all inhibited $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathsf{V I})$ removal, suggesting the unfavorable use of the ZIF-8- EGCG in salted waters. On the contrary, the natural cations (i.e. $\operatorname{K}^{+}$ , ${\mathsf{C a}}^{2+}$ , $\mathsf{N a}^{+}$ and $\mathsf{M g}^{2+}$ ) at very low concentrations enhanced the adsorption of Cd(II), $\mathsf{C u}(\mathrm{II}).$ and Ni(II). Therefore, the use of ZIF-8- EGCG may be more efficient in natural fresh water system where the background concentrations of common cations are relatively low. This study may shed light on the related studies pertaining to understanding the complex interactions of co-existing ions on MOF-related materials in water treatment.
